{
 "summary": "The Lean theorem asserts that some finitely presented group G is not sofic. Soficity is the standard local definition: for every finite F ⊆ G and every 0<ε<1 there is a map G → Sym(n), n>0, with σ(1)=1, that is ε-multiplicative on F and moves almost all points for each nontrivial g ∈ F. The definitions look faithful. The main caveat: the Lean statement is a bare existential and never names L_{F2}(1,2)^×, so it does not formalize Theorem 1.1 as stated. It instead adds finite presentability, which the excerpt does not mention.",
 "glossary": [
  {"decl": "normalizedHamming", "line": 7, "plain": "d_H(p,q) = |{y : p(y) ≠ q(y)}| / |Y| for permutations p, q of a finite set Y", "standard_concept": "normalized Hamming distance on Sym(n)", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "Division by 0 occurs only for empty Y. Every use is on Fin size with size > 0."},
  {"decl": "PermutationModel", "line": 11, "plain": "A positive integer n together with a map σ: G → Sym(n) satisfying σ(1)=1", "standard_concept": "a map G → Sym(n) used in a sofic approximation", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "The extra condition σ(1)=1 is harmless. Resetting σ(1) to 1 can only improve the approximation inequalities."},
  {"decl": "GoodOn", "line": 17, "plain": "For all g,h ∈ F: d_H(σ(gh), σ(g)σ(h)) < ε. For all g ∈ F with g ≠ 1: d_H(σ(g), 1) > 1 − ε.", "standard_concept": "(F,ε)-approximation", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": ""},
  {"decl": "Sofic", "line": 24, "plain": "For every finite F ⊆ G and every 0 < ε < 1 there exists an (F,ε)-good permutation model", "standard_concept": "sofic group", "matches_standard": "yes", "note": "Restricting to ε < 1 is harmless, since smaller ε is the harder case."}
 ],
